The U.S. [[Colorado|State of Colorado]]  has 273 [[municipal corporation|active municipalities]], comprising 198 [[town]]s, 73 [[City|cities]], and two [[Consolidated city-county|consolidated city and county governments]].<ref name=MuniIncCO>{{cite web|url=https://dola.colorado.gov/dlg_lgis_ui_pu/publicMunicipalities.jsf|title=Active Colorado Municipalities|publisher=[[Colorado Department of Local Affairs|Department of Local Affairs]]|year=2025|access-date=March 18, 2025}}</ref><ref name=Colorado_incplace>{{cite web|url=https://tigerweb.geo.census.gov/tigerwebmain/Files/tab20/tigerweb_tab20_incplace_co.html|title=State of Colorado Incorporated Places - Current/TAB20 - Data as of January 1, 2020|publisher=[[United States Census Bureau]]|date=January 1, 2020|access-date=October 24, 2023}}</ref> The [[Denver|City and County of Denver]], the [[List of capitals in the United States#State capitals|state capital]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sporcle.com/blog/2020/01/why-is-denver-the-capital-of-colorado/|title=Why Is Denver the Capital of Colorado?|work=Sporkle Blog|date=January 21, 2020|access-date=September 4, 2022}}</ref> is the oldest municipality in Colorado. On December 3, 1859, the extralegal [[Territory of Jefferson]] granted a charter to the consolidated [[History of Denver|City of Denver, Auraria, and Highland]].<ref name="Denver City">{{cite book|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=kBc4AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA190|chapter=To Charter and Consolidate the Towns of Denver, Auraria and Highland|title=Provisional Laws and Joint Resolutions of the General Assembly of Jefferson Territory|publisher=General Assembly of the Provisional Government of the Territory of Jefferson|date=December 3, 1859|access-date=November 7, 2024}}</ref> The [[Keystone, Colorado|Town of Keystone]], incorporated on February 8, 2024, is the newest Colorado municipality.

Colorado municipalities range in population from the City and County of Denver with a 2020 population of 715,522, to the [[Carbonate, Colorado|Town of Carbonate]], which has had no year-round population since the [[1890 United States census|1890 Census]] due to its severe winter weather and difficult access.{{efn|name=most recent|For the most recent population estimates, see the [[List of Colorado municipalities by population]].}}<ref name=Census/><ref name=Carbonate>{{cite web|url=https://www.postindependent.com/news/local/yes-thats-a-town-by-9-0-vote/|title=Yes, that's a town — by 9-0 vote|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170306212623/https://www.postindependent.com/news/local/yes-thats-a-town-by-9-0-vote/ |archive-date= March 6, 2017|first=John|last=Stroud|newspaper= [[Glenwood Springs Post Independent]]|date=November 8, 2014|accessdate=May 10, 2021}}</ref> The [[Black Hawk, Colorado|City of Black Hawk]] with a 2020 population of 127 is the least populous Colorado city, while the [[Castle Rock, Colorado|Town of Castle Rock]] with a 2020 population of 73,158 is the most populous Colorado town.<ref name=MuniIncCO/><ref name=Census/>

At the [[2020 United States census|2020 United States Census]], 4,299,942 of the 5,773,714 Colorado residents (74.47%) lived in one of the 271 municipalities active at the time.{{efn|name=exclude|The 2020 statistics exclude the [[Carbonate, Colorado|Town of Carbonate]] and the [[Keystone, Colorado|Town of Keystone]] which were not active in 2020.}}<ref name=Census>{{cite web|url=https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/dashboard/CO/|title=QuickFacts for Colorado|publisher=[[United States Census Bureau]]|access-date=September 4, 2022}}</ref> Another 714,417 residents (12.37%) lived in one of the 210 [[List of census-designated places in Colorado|census-designated places]], while the remaining 759,355 residents (13.15%) lived in the many rural and mountainous areas of the state.<ref name=Census/>

In 2020, only {{convert|5097|km2|mi2|0|order=flip}} of Colorado's {{convert|268431|km2|mi2|order=flip}} of land area (1.90%) were incorporated in the 271 active municipalities. The [[Colorado Springs, Colorado|City of Colorado Springs]] with {{convert|506|km2|mi2|order=flip}} of land area was the most extensive municipality, while the [[Sawpit, Colorado|Town of Sawpit]] with {{convert|0.078|km2|acre|order=flip}} of land area was the least extensive.<ref name=COmuni2020>{{cite web|url=https://tigerweb.geo.census.gov/tigerwebmain/Files/tab20/tigerweb_tab20_incplace_co.html|title=State of Colorado Incorporated Places - Current/TAB20 - Data as of January 1, 2020|publisher=[[United States Census Bureau]]|date=January 1, 2020|access-date=August 27, 2022}}</ref> The [[Glendale, Colorado|City of Glendale]] with a 2020 population density of 8,117 residents per square mile (3,134/km<sup>2</sup>) was the most densely populated municipality, while the [[Bonanza, Colorado|Town of Bonanza]] with a 2020 population density of 38 residents per square mile (15/km<sup>2</sup>) was the least densely populated municipality after Carbonate.
