==Municipal government ==
Colorado municipalities operate under various types of municipal governing authority &mdash; [[#Consolidated city and county|consolidated city and county]], [[#Home rule municipality|home rule municipality]] (which may be either a city and county, city, or a town), [[#Statutory city|statutory city]], [[#Statutory town|statutory town]], and [[#Territorial charter municipality|territorial charter municipality]].<ref name=MuniIncCO/> State law makes relatively few distinctions between a city and a town. The charter of a home rule municipality may designate either a city or town municipal title. In general, cities are more populous than towns, although long-term population changes may skew this considerably, as illustrated by the [[Black Hawk, Colorado|City of Black Hawk]] and the [[Castle Rock, Colorado|Town of Castle Rock]].

Neither [[Village (United States)|village]] nor [[civil township]] is a type of civil division in the State of Colorado. The cities of [[Cherry Hills Village, Colorado|Cherry Hills Village]] and [[Greenwood Village, Colorado|Greenwood Village]] and the towns of [[Log Lane Village, Colorado|Log Lane Village]], [[Mountain Village, Colorado|Mountain Village]], and [[Snowmass Village, Colorado|Snowmass Village]] have the word "village" at the end of their names.<ref name=MuniIncCO/>

Nineteen Colorado municipalities extend into two counties, while two cities &ndash; [[Aurora, Colorado|Aurora]] and [[Littleton, Colorado|Littleton]] &ndash; extend into three counties.<ref name=MuniIncCO/>

===Consolidated city and county===
In the State of Colorado, only Denver and [[Broomfield, Colorado|Broomfield]] have consolidated city and county governments. The City and County of Denver operates under Article XX, Section 4 of the [[Constitution of Colorado|Constitution of the State of Colorado]]; and Title 30, Article 11, Section 101 of the [[Colorado Revised Statutes]]. Denver has an elected [[Mayor#United States|mayor]] and a [[Municipal council#United States|city council]] of 13 members with 11 members elected from council districts and two members elected at large. The City and County of Broomfield operates under Article XX, Sections 10–13 of the Constitution of the State of Colorado. Broomfield has an appointed [[city manager|city and county manager]], an elected mayor, and a city council of 11 members composed of the mayor and two members elected from each of five wards.

===Home rule municipality===
As of 2025, Colorado has 108 cities and towns that are home rule municipalities,<ref name=CountiesCO/><ref>{{cite web | title=CML Member Directory | url=https://www.cml.org/utility-pages/cml-member-directory }}</ref> which are [[self-governing]] under Article 20 of the Constitution of the State of Colorado; Title 31, Article 1, Section 202 of the Colorado Revised Statutes; and the home rule charter of each municipality. The state-authorized home rule charter determines the form of government. Home rule gives local municipalities the power to make legislation relevant to their areas, exercising control over issues of local concern while minimizing state intervention in municipal affairs.<ref name="OP 2014-01-29">{{Cite news |last1=Block |first1=Sheridan |last2=Tiedje |first2=Bill |date=January 29, 2014 |title=COLORADO Comparing home rule and statutory municipalities |url=https://www.ouraynews.com/colorado-comparing-home-rule-and-statutory-municipalities |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190713174027/https://www.ouraynews.com/colorado-comparing-home-rule-and-statutory-municipalities |archive-date=2019-07-13 |work=Ouray Plaindealer}}</ref>

===Statutory town===
Colorado has 153 statutory towns that operate under Title 31, Article 1, Section 203 and Article 4, Part 3 of the Colorado Revised Statutes. The statutory town of [[Creede, Colorado|Creede]] uses the official title "City of Creede".<ref name=MuniIncCO/> Likewise, [[Garden City, Colorado|Garden City]], [[Lake City, Colorado|Lake City]], [[Orchard City, Colorado|Orchard City]], and [[Sugar City, Colorado|Sugar City]] are legally statutory towns and "city" is only a part of their names.<ref name=MuniIncCO/>

Statutory towns have an elected mayor and a [[Municipal council#United States|board of trustees]] composed of the mayor and four or six additional members elected at large. Colorado statutory cities and towns are limited to exercising powers that are granted by the state and are subject to provisions and limitations imposed by the state{{r|OP 2014-01-29}} and are similar to villages in other states such as the [[Administrative divisions of New York (state)#Village|villages of the State of New York]].

===Territorial charter municipality===
The [[Georgetown, Colorado|Town of Georgetown]] is the only municipality that still operates under a charter granted by the [[Colorado Territory|Territory of Colorado]]. The town operates under Article 14, Section 13 of the Charter & Constitution of the Colorado Territory enacted on January 28, 1868. The town mayor is called the police judge, and the town council is called the [[board of selectmen]].<ref name="FirstSettlers">{{cite web |url=http://www.townofgeorgetown.us/The%20first%20settlers%20came%20to%20the%20Georgetown%20area%20in%201859.pdf|title=First settlers came to the Georgetown are in 1859|access-date=August 29, 2022}}</ref>

