!scope=row style="background:#FFBBBB;"|[[Denver]] {{hash-tag|alt=State capital}}{{efn|The [[Denver|City and County of Denver]] is the [[List of United States cities by population|19th most populous U.S. municipality]].<ref name=USmuni/>}}{{efn|The [[Denver|City and County of Denver]] is the [[principal city]] of the [[Denver-Aurora-Centennial, CO Metropolitan Statistical Area]] and the [[Denver-Aurora-Greeley, CO Combined Statistical Area]].<ref name="OMB_23-01">{{cite web|subject=Revised Delineations of Metropolitan Statistical Areas, Micropolitan Statistical Areas, and Combined Statistical Areas, and Guidance on Uses of the Delineations of These Areas|title=OMB BULLETIN NO. 23-01|url=https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/OMB-Bulletin-23-01.pdf|publisher=[[Office of Management and Budget]]|date=July 21, 2023|access-date=May 20, 2025}}</ref>}}{{efn|name=Denver|On August 24, 1859, voters of the [[Pike's Peak Gold Rush|Pike's Peak mining region]] approved the formation of the [[Provisional Government of the Territory of Jefferson]] with [[Denver City, Jefferson Territory|Denver City]] as the territorial capital.<ref name="Jefferson Territory">{{cite journal|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=hkkRAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA16|journal=The University of Colorado Studies: General Series A|publisher=[[University of Colorado]]|location=[[Boulder, Colorado]]|pages=15–18|author=Frederic L. Paxson |title=The Territory of Jefferson: A Spontaneous Commonwealth|year=1906|volume=3|editor=Francis Ramaley|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.historycolorado.org%2Fsites%2Fdefault%2Ffiles%2Fmedia%2Fdocument%2F2018%2FColoradoMagazine_v12n4_July1935.pdf|title=The Model of Auraria-Denver of 1860|work=[[History Colorado|The Colorado Magazine]]|publisher=[[History Colorado|Colorado Historical Society]]|date=July 1935|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref> On November 28, 1859, the extralegal territory created 12 counties, with Denver City as the seat of [[Arrappahoe County, Jefferson Territory]].<ref name=JT>{{cite book|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=kBc4AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA190|chapter=An Act Defining the Boundaries of Counties and for other purposes|title=Provisional Laws and Joint Resolutions of the General Assembly of Jefferson Territory|publisher=General Assembly of the Provisional Government of the Territory of Jefferson|date=November 28, 1859|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref> On December 3, 1859, the Jefferson Territory granted a charter to the consolidated [[History of Denver|City of Denver, Auraria, and Highland]], still commonly known as Denver City.<ref name="Denver City"/> On November 13, 1860, the Jefferson Territory moved its capital to [[Golden City, Jefferson Territory|Golden City]]. On February 28, 1861, [[President of the United States|U.S. President]] [[James Buchanan]] signed [[Colorado Organic Act|An Act to provide a temporary Government for the Territory of Colorado]], which designated Denver City as the original capital of the [[Colorado Territory]].<ref name="Colorado Origin Act">{{cite web|url=https://www.loc.gov/law/help/statutes-at-large/36th-congress/session-2/c36s2ch59.pdf|title=An Act To provide a temporary Government for the Territory of Colorado|author=Thirty-sixth United States Congress|website=[[Library of Congress]] |author-link=36th United States Congress|date=February 28, 1861|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref> On June 6, 1861, the Jefferson Territory officially yielded to the new territory. On November 1, 1861, the Colorado Territory created 17 original counties, with Denver City as the original seat of [[Arapahoe County, Colorado|Arapahoe County]]. On November 7, 1861, the Colorado Territory reincorporated the city as the [[History of Denver|City of Denver City]].<ref>{{cite book|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=kBc4AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA190|chapter=An Act Defining the Boundaries of Counties and for other purposes|title=Provisional Laws and Joint Resolutions of the General Assembly of Jefferson Territory|publisher=General Assembly of the Territory of Jefferson|date=November 28, 1859|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref> On July 7, 1862, the Colorado Territory moved its capital to [[Colorado City, Colorado Territory|Colorado City]]. On February 13, 1866, the city shortened its name to the [[Denver|City of Denver]].<ref name=CPO>{{cite book|title=Colorado Post Offices 1859-1989|first1=William H.|last1=Bauer|first2=James L.|last2=Ozment|first3=John H.|last3=Willard|date=1990|publisher=[[Colorado Railroad Museum|Colorado Railroad Historical Foundation]]|location=[[Golden, Colorado]]|isbn=0-918654-42-4}}</ref> Denver served again as the capital of the Colorado Territory from December 9, 1867, until statehood on August 1, 1876, when Denver became the (first and only) capital of the [[Colorado|State of Colorado]]. The [[History of Denver|City and County of Denver]] was [[consolidated city-county|consolidated]] on December 1, 1902.<ref name=MuniIncCO/>}}{{efn|name=mile_high|[[Denver]] has retained its "Mile-High City" elevation of {{convert|5280|ft|3}} despite advances in scientific measurement. When the [[U.S. National Geodetic Survey|National Geodetic Survey]] makes adjustments to the [[North American Vertical Datum of 1988|North American Vertical Datum (NAVD)]], the [[Colorado|State of Colorado]] moves the official mile-high elevation point up or down the west steps of the [[Colorado State Capitol]] to reflect the changes. Most of Denver actually lies above this elevation.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.5280.com/2015/05/only-in-colorado-the-capitols-mile-high-steps/|title=Only in Colorado: The Capitol's Mile-High Step(s)|work=[[5280 Magazine]]|first=Terri|last=Cook|date=May 26, 2015|access-date=August 27, 2022}}</ref>}}
|[[List of municipalities in Colorado#Consolidated city and county|Consolidated city and county]]
